===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
Originally written as {{zh-l|*賖}}, which is a {{Han compound|貝|余|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=shell|nocap=1}} – to buy or sell on credit.

In the era of Middle Chinese, a vulgar form {{zh-l|*𧶟}}, also a {{liushu|4|nocap=1}} with phonetic component {{ltc-l|舍}} that was more similar-sounding to the character, once appeared but never became popularized.

The current form {{lang|zh|賒}} appeared as a vulgar form at the Ming-Qing transition and is a {{liushu|4|nocap=1}} with phonetic component {{zh-l|*佘}} that is similar-sounding to the character.

===Etymology===
According to STEDT, from {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*g/m/s-laj ~ r/s-lej ~ b-rej||to change, exchange, buy}} (whence also {{och-l|移|change, alter}} & {{och-l|易|id=2|change, exchange}}); if so, Chinese vocalism might have been influenced by superficially similar words like {{och-l|舍|id=1|to bestow, to give}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). 

{{och-l|商}} is its derivative with terminative suffix *''-ŋ''; {{och-l|貰|tr=}}, minimally reconstructed as *''lhas'' ~ *''m-las'', is another with *''-s'' > *''-h'' extrovert suffix (ibid.).

{{cog|th|แลก}}'s possible relation to {{och-l|賒}} (ibid.) is through its cognate {{och-l|易}}, wherefrom arose {{cog|th|แลก}}, {{cog|lo|ແລກ}}, {{cog|shn|လႅၵ်ႈ}}, {{cog|tdd|ᥘᥦᥐ}}, & {{cog|aho|𑜎𑜢𑜀𑜫}} ({{zh-ref|Pittayaporn, 2014}})

Lastly, {{zh-ref|Bodman (1980)}}) proposes cognacy to {{cog|bo|གྱར་བ||to borrow, to lend, to hire}}.

