===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

In oracle bone script, an {{liushu|ic|nocap=yes}}: a person {{och-l|*人}} in a building {{och-l|*宀}} – a guest. Bronze inscriptions added {{och-l|*貝|money; valuables}}, signifying gifts.

Variants that replaced {{och-l|*人}} with {{och-l|万}} were also common, and posited to be the original form of {{och-l|丏}}, which would also act as the character's phonetic component, which has corrupted into ⿱一𣥂 in the modern form.

''{{w|Shuowen}}'': {{Han compound|丏|貝|alt1=𡧍|ls=psc|c1=p|c2=s}}.

===Etymology 1===
From n-nominalization of {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*pi|t=to give}}. Cognate with {{och-l|嬪|to become a bride}}, which is an endopassive of {{och-l|賓|''lit'', "to be received like a guest"}}. See {{och-l|畀|to give}} for more cognates.

